id: P-framework-bug-surface
name: Surface a framework bug detected by an agent
description: When an MCP tool misbehaves and the cause is in framework code (not project state), file a tagged framework-bug task with two-location evidence instead of hand-editing project files
trigger:
  - framework bug
  - mcp tool wrong
  - tool returns missing
  - writer reader mismatch
  - tool contract violated
  - aspect check missing anchor
  - portal gate not persisted
tags:
  - framework
  - mcp
  - bug
  - agent-protocol
  - calibration
symbols: []
exemplar: .paradigm/research/path-bug-and-agent-protocol-analysis.md
steps:
  - action: detect
    notes: |
      Trigger conditions — file a framework-bug task when ANY of the following hold:
      - An MCP write tool reported success but the corresponding read tool reports the
        data as missing or wrong on the next call (canonical case: paradigm_purpose_add_aspect
        ↔ paradigm_aspect_check, the v6.0.4 path-resolution bug).
      - An MCP tool's behavior contradicts its documented contract (.paradigm/specs or
        the tool description in packages/paradigm-mcp/src/tools/*).
      - You can name TWO code locations — a writer file:line and a reader file:line —
        that demonstrate the divergence in convention.
  - action: gather-evidence
    notes: |
      Calibration gate (non-negotiable):
      The report MUST attach two file:line citations:
        - writer file:line — where the data is produced/persisted
        - reader file:line — where the data is consumed and disagrees
      Without both citations, downgrade to a plain task (no `framework-bug` tag).
      This is the same evidentiary bar a human bug report would clear. Without it,
      signal-to-noise collapses as every agent surprise becomes a "framework bug."
  - action: identify-domain
    notes: |
      Domain ownership map (per Rune, agent-owned enforcement TD-2026-04-25-417):
      Ownership follows the broken tool's domain. Reject the meta-archetype option —
      every archetype already knows the semantics of its own tools well enough to
      spot a framework defect; the protocol just gives them a place to file it.

        Compliance tools (paradigm_aspect_check, paradigm_drift_*)        → Rune
        Security tools (paradigm_portal_*, gate verification)             → Aegis
        Learning tools (paradigm_notebook_*, nomination)                  → Scholar + Loid
        Navigation tools (paradigm_status, paradigm_navigate)             → Cid
        Other / cross-cutting                                             → first archetype to surface owns triage

      When the tool's domain crosses archetypes, the first archetype to surface owns
      triage and adds the second as reviewer (same partner pattern as Scholar+Sheila).
  - action: file-task
    target: paradigm_task_create
    notes: |
      Option α (current, v6.0.5+):
        paradigm_task_create({
          blurb: "FRAMEWORK BUG: <one-line summary>. Writer at <file:line>; reader at <file:line>; symptom: <what user/agent sees>",
          priority: "high",
          tags: ["framework-bug", "<#affected-tool>", "<archetype-domain>"]
        })

      Notes:
      - The blurb's "Writer at … reader at …" phrasing satisfies the calibration gate
        in machine-readable form — triagers can grep for it.
      - Tag the affected tool symbol (e.g., `paradigm_aspect_check`) so the task
        threads onto Rune's coverage view.
      - Tag the archetype domain (`compliance`, `security`, `learning`, `navigation`)
        so the right triage owner is paged.
  - action: continue-session
    notes: |
      Filing the task does NOT block the session. The agent continues its current
      task, working around the framework bug without modifying .purpose, portal.yaml,
      or other Paradigm-owned files. The framework-bug task is a signal to the user
      and triage owners, not a halt instruction.
  - action: forbidden
    notes: |
      What NOT to do — antipatterns the protocol exists to prevent:
      - Hand-edit the affected `.purpose` file to make the broken read "look right".
      - Hand-edit `portal.yaml` to work around a portal-tool bug.
      - Acknowledge the bug only in your own session lore ("this exists in this project too")
        without filing upstream — converts a framework defect into a project-state
        acknowledgment, killing upstream signal. The blast radius of a framework bug
        is every adopter; the agent's blast radius is one project. Telemetry mismatch.
      - File without two-location evidence — the calibration gate exists for a reason.
  - action: v6-1-escalation
    notes: |
      Coming in v6.1 Sprint 1 (Option γ — soft-block primitive):
        paradigm_propose_block({
          claimant: 'framework',
          reason: '<framework-bug task id> blocks correctness',
          evidence: { writer: '<file:line>', reader: '<file:line>' }
        })

      The block persists across session resumes until upstream fixes (or the user
      overrides it locally). Use γ on top of α only when the framework bug actively
      misleads current decisions, not for inconvenience-only cases.

      Option β (`paradigm_framework_issue` dedicated tool) stays deferred until
      α + γ field data shows existing primitives are insufficient.
  - action: verify
    notes: |
      Self-check before declaring the report filed:
      - Both file:line citations present in the blurb? (writer:line + reader:line)
      - Tag set includes `framework-bug` and the archetype-domain tag?
      - You did NOT modify .purpose, portal.yaml, or other Paradigm-owned files?
      - Session continued past the report (not blocked on the bug)?
      If any answer is "no", revise before submitting.
